1
00:00:01,283 --> 00:00:04,172
On 26 December 2004,

2
00:00:04,172 --> 00:00:07,096
a magnitude 9.1 earthquake broke

3
00:00:07,096 --> 00:00:08,821
the seafloor off Sumatra.

4
00:00:10,127 --> 00:00:11,743
The tsunami took 2 hours

5
00:00:11,743 --> 00:00:13,889
36 minutes to reach Chennai,

6
00:00:13,889 --> 00:00:16,193
and almost three hours to reach Colombo.

7
00:00:16,861 --> 00:00:19,385
The Indian Ocean had no warning system.

8
00:00:19,385 --> 00:00:21,994
More than 227,000 people died.

9
00:00:23,316 --> 00:00:24,566
This is LeadTime.

10
00:00:24,566 --> 00:00:26,146
It asks one question:

11
00:00:26,146 --> 00:00:28,532
how many minutes does a coastal city get to run?

12
00:00:29,416 --> 00:00:31,432
Replay 2004,

13
00:00:31,432 --> 00:00:33,720
and a shallow-water solver carries the

14
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:36,385
wave across the real ocean floor,

15
00:00:36,385 --> 00:00:37,439
live in your browser.

16
00:00:38,027 --> 00:00:41,082
Over deep water it moves as fast as a jet,

17
00:00:41,082 --> 00:00:42,744
and twelve hours of tsunami play

18
00:00:42,744 --> 00:00:43,975
out in under a minute.

19
00:00:44,827 --> 00:00:46,962
The seafloor bends it into beams.

20
00:00:46,962 --> 00:00:50,457
Here's where it hit hardest, and when, hour by hour.

21
00:00:51,363 --> 00:00:53,285
For 65 cities on the coast,

22
00:00:53,285 --> 00:00:55,042
you see when the wave arrives,

23
00:00:55,042 --> 00:00:56,438
and how many people live there.

24
00:00:57,027 --> 00:00:59,885
With no sensors, nobody gets a confirmed warning.

25
00:01:00,738 --> 00:01:01,000
Now you're the engineer.

26
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,089
Drop one deep-ocean sensor west of Sumatra,

27
00:01:05,089 --> 00:01:07,837
and 73.1 million people get at

28
00:01:07,837 --> 00:01:09,857
least fifteen minutes to evacuate.

29
00:01:10,461 --> 00:01:14,167
Chennai, with 6.7 million people, gets 101 minutes.

30
00:01:15,094 --> 00:01:17,311
The optimizer tests 15 possible

31
00:01:17,311 --> 00:01:19,222
earthquakes along the trenches,

32
00:01:19,222 --> 00:01:20,605
and places six sensors.

33
00:01:21,194 --> 00:01:24,697
The first warns about 53 million people per quake.

34
00:01:24,697 --> 00:01:26,162
The sixth adds nothing.

35
00:01:26,816 --> 00:01:29,492
Real networks also weigh cost and upkeep,

36
00:01:29,492 --> 00:01:31,478
but now you can see what each sensor buys.

37
00:01:32,494 --> 00:01:33,721
And the honest part:

38
00:01:33,721 --> 00:01:36,409
Banda Aceh is hit in 21 minutes.

39
00:01:36,409 --> 00:01:37,674
No sensor can save it.

40
00:01:38,316 --> 00:01:41,912
On nearby Simeulue Island, an oral tradition did.

41
00:01:41,912 --> 00:01:45,773
Only seven of more than 78,000 people died there.

42
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:48,353
Can you trust the physics?

43
00:01:48,353 --> 00:01:51,349
Against 13 tide gauges from 2004,

44
00:01:51,349 --> 00:01:54,356
our arrival times are off by 20 minutes on average,

45
00:01:54,356 --> 00:01:55,420
and mostly early.

46
00:01:56,061 --> 00:01:58,777
So the warning times you see err on the safe side.

47
00:01:59,956 --> 00:02:00,977
LeadTime.

48
00:02:00,977 --> 00:02:03,502
Free, open source, no login.

49
00:02:03,502 --> 00:02:04,656
Every minute counts.
